    "result_text": "Terms now count business days, and `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\n**How it works now.** `Holidays.dueBy(sheet, date, terms)` in `src/main/java/net/officefloor/hq/app/holiday/Holidays.java` starts at the day after the claim's date and counts forward, skipping Saturdays, Sundays and the sheet's holidays, until it reaches the terms-th business day. The old rule (date plus calendar days, then moved past a holiday) is gone. Every place that sets a due-by now uses the new rule:\n- adding a claim\n- quick add\n- paste (both the check and the add)\n- splitting a claim\n- the \"is this due-by in a closed period\" refusal checks\n\nOverdue, the late charge's 30-day boundaries and the period a claim belongs to all read the stored due-by, so they follow without any change. The screen just shows the due-by the server sends, so the front end only needed a doc comment updated.\n\n**Claims already on the sheet.** A new migration, `src/main/java/db/migration/V43__claim_business_day_due_by.java`, recomputes the due-by of every existing claim and its history rows, using the holidays on the sheet when it runs. Holidays added later don't touch an existing due-by, because the due-by stays stored once it's set. Flyway's log shows it applied, but the test database starts empty, so it ran over no claims. Its recalculation hasn't been tried on real data.\n\n**Decision for you:** the store never kept the terms each claim was fixed with, only its due-by, so the migration has to reconstruct them. It takes the calendar days from the claim's date to its due-by, minus any run of holidays just before the due-by (the days the old rule would have pushed it past). That's exact unless a holiday was added just before a claim's due-by after the claim was made: then the migration takes it as a skipped holiday and the recovered terms come out one short per such day. If you need exact values, the fix is to store the terms on each claim when it's made. I didn't add that because the request didn't ask for it.",
